# Fastify API Rules — GoodEpoch Cursor Rules Pack v1
# Applies to: Fastify 5.x on Node.js 22+
# Stack: Fastify 5, TypeScript 5.5+, Node.js 22 LTS

## Why Fastify Over Express

- Fastify is 2-3x faster than Express. Use it for any new API in 2026
- Schema-first validation with JSON Schema or zod via `@fastify/zod-type-provider`
- TypeBox integration for full type safety on request/response
- Plugin system prevents circular dependencies and handles async initialization

## Project Structure

```
src/
  plugins/          # Fastify plugins (db, auth, sensible)
  routes/           # Route definitions with schemas
  services/         # Business logic
  schemas/          # Shared TypeBox/Zod schemas
  hooks/            # Lifecycle hooks
  types/            # TypeScript types
  app.ts            # App factory function
  server.ts         # Entry point
```

## App Setup

```typescript
// app.ts — always export a factory, not a singleton
import Fastify from 'fastify'
import { TypeBoxTypeProvider } from '@fastify/type-provider-typebox'

export const buildApp = async () => {
  const app = Fastify({ logger: true }).withTypeProvider<TypeBoxTypeProvider>()
  
  await app.register(import('@fastify/sensible'))  // Adds error helpers
  await app.register(import('./plugins/db'))
  await app.register(import('./plugins/auth'))
  await app.register(import('./routes/users'), { prefix: '/api/users' })
  
  return app
}

// server.ts
const app = await buildApp()
await app.listen({ port: 3000, host: '0.0.0.0' })
```

## Schema-First Route Definitions

- Define request/response schemas inline using TypeBox:
  ```typescript
  import { Type } from '@sinclair/typebox'
  
  const CreateUserBody = Type.Object({
    email: Type.String({ format: 'email' }),
    name: Type.String({ minLength: 1, maxLength: 100 }),
  })
  
  const UserResponse = Type.Object({
    id: Type.String(),
    email: Type.String(),
    name: Type.String(),
    createdAt: Type.String({ format: 'date-time' }),
  })
  
  app.post('/', {
    schema: {
      body: CreateUserBody,
      response: { 201: UserResponse }
    }
  }, async (req, reply) => {
    const user = await userService.create(req.body)  // req.body is fully typed
    return reply.code(201).send(user)
  })
  ```
- Fastify validates and serializes automatically — no manual validation needed
- Response schema serialization is ~2x faster than JSON.stringify

## Plugin Pattern

- Every cross-cutting concern is a plugin:
  ```typescript
  // plugins/db.ts
  import fp from 'fastify-plugin'
  import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'
  
  export default fp(async (app) => {
    const db = drizzle(process.env.DATABASE_URL!)
    app.decorate('db', db)
    app.addHook('onClose', async () => { await db.$client.end() })
  }, { name: 'db' })
  
  // Extend types
  declare module 'fastify' {
    interface FastifyInstance { db: ReturnType<typeof drizzle> }
  }
  ```
- `fastify-plugin` (`fp`) unwraps the plugin scope so decorations are available app-wide
- Register plugins once, use everywhere via `app.db`, `app.redis`, etc.

## Error Handling

- Use `@fastify/sensible` for HTTP errors: `reply.badRequest()`, `reply.notFound()`, `reply.unauthorized()`
- Custom error handler:
  ```typescript
  app.setErrorHandler((error, request, reply) => {
    if (error.validation) {
      return reply.code(400).send({ error: 'VALIDATION_ERROR', details: error.validation })
    }
    request.log.error({ err: error }, 'Unhandled error')
    reply.code(error.statusCode ?? 500).send({ error: error.message })
  })
  ```
- Fastify catches async errors automatically — no try/catch needed in route handlers unless you want custom handling

## Authentication with @fastify/jwt

```typescript
// plugins/auth.ts
import fp from 'fastify-plugin'
import jwt from '@fastify/jwt'

export default fp(async (app) => {
  await app.register(jwt, { secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET! })
  
  app.decorate('authenticate', async (request: FastifyRequest, reply: FastifyReply) => {
    try {
      await request.jwtVerify()
    } catch {
      reply.unauthorized('Invalid or expired token')
    }
  })
})

// In routes
app.get('/profile', { onRequest: [app.authenticate] }, async (req, reply) => {
  return req.user  // typed by jwt payload declaration
})
```

## Hooks

- `onRequest`: auth, rate limiting (runs before parsing)
- `preHandler`: validation logic, authorization (runs after parsing)
- `onSend`: response transformation, headers
- `onClose`: cleanup (DB connections, caches)
- Use hooks for cross-cutting concerns, not for business logic

## Logging

- Fastify ships with `pino` built in — `request.log` for per-request logging
- Structured logging automatically includes `requestId`, method, url, statusCode, responseTime
- Never use `console.log` — use `app.log.info()` or `request.log.info()`
- In development, use `pino-pretty` for human-readable logs

## Performance Tips

- Keep plugins small and focused — Fastify optimizes per-route schemas at startup
- Use `Type.Strict()` in TypeBox schemas to strip unknown properties from responses
- `fastify-caching` or Redis for response caching on read-heavy endpoints
- Avoid JSON schema $ref in hot paths — inline schemas are faster
- Use `reply.raw` only when streaming large responses

## Testing

```typescript
// Use buildApp() factory in tests
import { buildApp } from '../src/app'

test('POST /users creates user', async () => {
  const app = await buildApp()
  const res = await app.inject({
    method: 'POST',
    url: '/api/users',
    payload: { email: 'test@example.com', name: 'Test' }
  })
  expect(res.statusCode).toBe(201)
  expect(res.json().email).toBe('test@example.com')
})
```
- `app.inject()` for in-process testing — no real HTTP, ultra fast
- Never start the actual server in tests
